## Entities freeze mid-animation when near the edge of view / partially culled, then fast-forward once fully visible

**Mod version:** entityculling-fabric-1.11.0-mc26.3
**Minecraft:** 26.3 (Fabric)
**Related mods:** none required to reproduce — happens with vanilla-ish entities (players, iron golems, cats, rabbits, etc.)

### Description

When a moving entity is near the edge of the camera's view — close to occluded/culled but not fully out of sight — its movement/animation appears to freeze in place. Once you look toward it enough that it becomes fully visible again, it "fast-forwards" and rapidly plays back all the movement it made while it was frozen, until it catches up to its real current position.

This affects basically any moving entity (tested with players, iron golems, cats, rabbits) and appears to be a regression introduced by the 26.3 update to EntityCulling, presumably related to Minecraft's newer submit/extract rendering architecture (`LevelExtractor.extractEntity`).

### Root cause

In `dev.tr7zw.entityculling.mixin.WorldRendererMixin`, the `extractEntityRedir` method — a `@Inject(method = "extractEntity", at = @At("HEAD"), cancellable = true)` — fully cancels the real `LevelExtractor.extractEntity()` call for culled entities and substitutes a minimal, hand-built `EntityRenderState`:

```java
@Inject(method = "extractEntity", at = @At("HEAD"), cancellable = true)
private void extractEntityRedir(Entity entity, float partialTick, CallbackInfoReturnable<EntityRenderState> cir) {
    if (EntityCullingModBase.instance.config.skipEntityCulling) {
        return;
    }
    Cullable cullable = (Cullable) entity;
    if (!cullable.isForcedVisible() && cullable.isCulled() && !NMSCullingHelper.ignoresCulling(entity)) {
        EntityCullingModBase.instance.skippedEntities++;
        EntityRenderState state = new EntityRenderState();
        state.entityType = EntityTypes.INTERACTION; // generic placeholder type
        state = processNametag(entity, partialTick, state);
        state.x = Mth.lerp(partialTick, entity.xOld, entity.getX());
        state.y = Mth.lerp(partialTick, entity.yOld, entity.getY());
        state.z = Mth.lerp(partialTick, entity.zOld, entity.getZ());
        state.isInvisible = true;
        cir.setReturnValue(state); // <-- cancels real extraction
        if (EntityCullingModBase.instance.debugCollector.isRunning()) {
            EntityCullingModBase.instance.debugCollector.addEntity(entity, false, false);
        }
        return;
    }
    EntityCullingModBase.instance.renderedEntities++;
    cullable.setOutOfCamera(false);
    if (EntityCullingModBase.instance.debugCollector.isRunning()) {
        EntityCullingModBase.instance.debugCollector.addEntity(entity, true, NMSCullingHelper.ignoresCulling(entity));
    }
}
```

Because real extraction never runs while an entity is culled, whatever per-frame interpolation/animation-state advancement normally happens inside vanilla's real `extractEntity()` (walk-cycle progress, limb swing, etc.) never advances during that time. The entity's animation state goes stale and only resumes once culling stops — which is exactly the "freeze, then fast-forward to catch up" behavior being reported.

Note this only affects the render-extraction path. The actual occlusion-culling decision (raycasting) and the optional tick-culling feature are unrelated code paths and are not implicated.

### Suggested fix

Don't skip real extraction for culled entities — let it always run so animation/interpolation state stays fresh — and instead mark the *real* resulting render state invisible afterward, via a second injection at `@At("RETURN")`:

```java
@Inject(method = "extractEntity", at = @At("HEAD"), cancellable = true)
private void extractEntityRedir(Entity entity, float partialTick, CallbackInfoReturnable<EntityRenderState> cir) {
    if (EntityCullingModBase.instance.config.skipEntityCulling) {
        return;
    }
    Cullable cullable = (Cullable) entity;
    if (!cullable.isForcedVisible() && cullable.isCulled() && !NMSCullingHelper.ignoresCulling(entity)) {
        // Culled: bookkeeping only. Do NOT cancel — let vanilla's real extraction run so
        // per-frame interpolation/animation state keeps advancing even while hidden.
        EntityCullingModBase.instance.skippedEntities++;
        if (EntityCullingModBase.instance.debugCollector.isRunning()) {
            EntityCullingModBase.instance.debugCollector.addEntity(entity, false, false);
        }
        return;
    }
    EntityCullingModBase.instance.renderedEntities++;
    cullable.setOutOfCamera(false);
    if (EntityCullingModBase.instance.debugCollector.isRunning()) {
        EntityCullingModBase.instance.debugCollector.addEntity(entity, true, NMSCullingHelper.ignoresCulling(entity));
    }
}

@Inject(method = "extractEntity", at = @At("RETURN"))
private void extractEntityForceInvisible(Entity entity, float partialTick, CallbackInfoReturnable<EntityRenderState> cir) {
    if (EntityCullingModBase.instance.config.skipEntityCulling) {
        return;
    }
    Cullable cullable = (Cullable) entity;
    if (cullable.isForcedVisible() || !cullable.isCulled() || NMSCullingHelper.ignoresCulling(entity)) {
        return;
    }
    Object rv = cir.getReturnValue();
    if (rv instanceof EntityRenderState state) {
        state.isInvisible = true;
    }
}
```

This preserves the actual performance win (culled entities still get `isInvisible = true`, so the expensive draw/submit step still skips them — same mechanism vanilla uses for the Invisibility effect) while fixing the staleness bug, at the cost of a small amount of extra per-frame CPU work (interpolation/animation bookkeeping) for culled entities that was previously skipped.

### Testing

I don't have your source tree, so the above is reconstructed from decompiling the compiled mixin class and reasoned about from there — I validated it by binary-patching `WorldRendererMixin.class` directly (ASM bytecode rewrite implementing exactly the logic above) and testing in-game. Confirmed:
- The freeze/fast-forward behavior is gone for players, iron golems, cats, and rabbits near the edge of view.

Happy to share the patched jar or the exact bytecode diff if that's useful as a reference while implementing this against the real source.

